 2
 
This report analyzes an attack that uses a phishing email impersonating the information 
technology (IT) department, a pretexting phone call, and a counterfeit login portal to steal an 
employee’s credentials at a mid-sized company. The attack exploits trust in internal 
communication. The most effective defense is independent verification of identity and requests. 
Description of the Scenario and Tactics Used 
The attack combines reconnaissance, spear phishing, spoofing, and pretexting 
(TryHackMe, n.d.-a). The attacker may have studied the company’s IT emails and support portal 
to create look-alike versions. The spoofed email impersonates IT and announces an urgent 
security update, using authority, urgency, and fear to prompt compliance. 
The email prepares the employee for the follow-up call, where the attacker uses 
pretexting and voice phishing, posing as a support technician who needs the employee’s 
password to complete the update. The email and call appear to confirm each other, although both 
come from the attacker. The email’s link leads to a spoofed portal on a look-alike domain, a 
credential-harvesting technique (TryHackMe, n.d.-b, n.d.-e). The call and portal give the attacker 
two chances to capture the same credentials, which the attacker then uses to log in to the internal 
network and look for further vulnerabilities. 
Evaluation of the Attack’s Effectiveness and Potential Impact 
The attack can be effective because employees routinely receive IT notices and support 
calls, and each channel makes the other seem legitimate. It remains plausible wherever accounts 
rely on passwords alone. An unfamiliar caller number might raise suspicion, but caller ID can be 
falsified (Federal Communications Commission [FCC], n.d.). Attackers also continue to 
impersonate IT help desk staff by phone and bypass multifactor authentication (MFA) through 
repeated push notifications (Cybersecurity and Infrastructure Security Agency [CISA], 2025). 
 
 
 

 
 3
 
Some employees are easier targets than others. The scenario does not state the 
employee’s tenure, which may matter. Westholt and Straub (2025) found that each additional 
year of employment made employees about 7% less likely to click a phishing link and 8% less 
likely to enter credentials. New hires may face additional risk because setup requests are routine. 
The attack can still fail if the employee notices an external-sender banner or mismatched domain, 
questions why IT needs a password, or verifies the call through the company directory. 
The impact depends on the account’s permissions and how quickly the intrusion is 
detected. Unauthorized access could expose customer records, employee information, or 
intellectual property, leading to fraud, recovery costs, and damaged customer trust. If the 
employee reuses the password, the attacker could also try it on other services through credential 
stuffing (TryHackMe, n.d.-a). Containment could disrupt operations as accounts are disabled or 
access to affected systems is restricted. 
Suggestions for Improvements or Alternative Approaches 
The attack would be harder to detect if the email and call matched the company’s real 
language and support procedures (TryHackMe, n.d.-b). The attacker could have the employee 
enter the password on the cloned portal rather than say it aloud or include an attacker-controlled 
“verification” number in the email. Alternative approaches include SMS phishing, vendor 
impersonation, or a password-reset pretext aimed at the help desk. 
Detection and Prevention Recommendations 
Training should cover email and phone deception and begin during onboarding. 
Employees should check sender addresses and link destinations or go directly to the real site 
(TryHackMe, n.d.-a). New hires should practice hanging up on a simulated IT call and calling 
back through the directory, as CISA (2024) recommends for suspected impersonation calls. 
 
 
 

 
 4
 
Policy should state that IT will never request a password or MFA code, since clearly defined 
processes help employees recognize fraudulent requests (National Cyber Security Centre 
[NCSC], n.d.). A phishing reporting button and a blameless reporting culture encourages fast  
reporting (NCSC, n.d.). 
Technical controls should include email filtering, external-sender warnings, and deploy 
Sender Policy Framework and DomainKeys Identified Mail with an enforced Domain-based 
Message Authentication, Reporting, and Conformance policy. These limit spoofing of the 
company’s own domain but not look-alike domains (TryHackMe, n.d.-f). Analysts should 
examine headers and link targets in reported messages (TryHackMe, n.d.-c, n.d.-d). The 
company should require phishing-resistant MFA, such as FIDO2 security keys or passkeys, 
which will not authenticate to a fake portal (CISA, n.d.; National Institute of Standards and 
Technology [NIST], 2025). Least privilege and segmentation limit the damage. If credentials are 
disclosed, the company should reset the password, revoke active sessions, and review access 
logs. 
Conclusion 
This attack works by making attacker-controlled messages appear to confirm one another, 
particularly when employees are unfamiliar with support procedures. Each stage depends on the 
employee trusting an email or caller without checking it independently. As attacks become more 
polished, the company cannot rely on employees noticing mistakes. Its best defenses are an 
established verification process, phishing-resistant authentication that makes a stolen password 
insufficient, and rapid reporting and containment when an attack still succeeds. 
 
 
 
 
 

 
